A differential fuzzer against openssl asn1parse, and the two defects it found

The corruption sweep this replaces was 00 and FF at every offset of one
certificate: systematic, but it never produces a structurally valid file
that is semantically strange, so almost every input it makes is refused by
both programs before a value is printed. t/fuzz.py mutates the whole corpus
instead. Five of its eight mutators are blind byte edits; the other three
retag an element, change a byte inside a primitive's content, or swap two
siblings, and all three leave every length in the file correct. That is
what gets a mutant as far as a comparison, and the counts say so: swapping
siblings reached a comparison 168 times out of 168 and a content byte 201
of 240, against 0 of 61 for truncation.

What the oracle proved. Over 10,000 mutants at three seeds, 6,390 were
parsed by both programs and compared byte for byte, with 0 mismatches and
0 crashes. The one wrong answer it found is the OID table: 30 mutants
produced a valid file where openssl has a name and derx prints the dotted
form. Those are counted on their own line and not inside the mismatch
count, which is a judgement recorded in README.md rather than made
silently: they are one missing table, and growing it from objects.txt is
already a planned item. The test for that category is narrow, every
differing line must be an OBJECT line identical in offset, depth, header
length and length with derx's value purely numeric and openssl's not, so a
wrong name or wrong digits is still a mismatch and still fails.

Refusals are counted apart from wrong answers, because merging them is how
a reader that reads DER gets marked wrong for doing its job: 475 of the
seed-7 mutants were refused by derx and accepted by openssl, and the run
prints the diagnostics grouped, which is the list of DER rules the run
actually exercised. The opposite direction, derx accepting what openssl
refuses, is a failure, and it was 0 until a defect was planted.

Vacuity is the thing a fuzzer fails at quietly, so the count that reaches a
comparison is asserted, not just the count that differs. make test runs 600
mutants and requires at least 250 of them to have been parsed by both
sides; 365 are. A run where every mutant was rejected before parsing has
tested nothing and must not report a pass.

Reproducibility: mutant i is a pure function of (seed, i), there is no wall
clock anywhere in the file, every failure is written out whole and shrunk,
and the scratch directory is a per-run mkdtemp under t/ rather than the
fixed t/tmp the suite owns.

Two defects found, one in the code and one in the harness.

slurp() allocated 64 KiB and never gave the slack back, so for a 1 KiB
certificate every byte a parser bug might read past the last content octet
was inside a live allocation. make test-asan could not see that whole class
of overread. The fuzzer found it by catching a planted missing bounds check
as "derx accepted 33 files openssl refused" while ASan reported nothing on
all 600 mutants. slurp() now reallocs down to the exact size and the same
plant produces 31 ASan aborts. The sanitizer gate had never been watched to
fail on this, so its reach was a guess.

The fuzzer's own determinism assertion was checking its own echo. It ran
the fuzzer twice at one seed and once at another and compared the logs, and
it passed with --seed deliberately ignored, because the logs still differ
on the line that prints the seed back. It compares SHA-256 digests of the
mutants now, in three claims: 40 mutants are produced, --start 30 --count
10 is byte-identical to the last ten of them, which is the promise that a
saved failure replays by index, and seeds 2 and 3 do not agree. Both ways
of breaking the seed wiring are caught; both were watched to fail.

Mutation-tested against four defects planted in derx.c one at a time, all
four caught at the seed and count make test uses: an off-by-one in the
two's-complement negation (2 mismatches), a misspelled tag-21 name (4
mismatches), the non-minimal INTEGER rule deleted (3 mismatches), and the
content-length bounds check deleted (31 crashes and 31 files accepted that
openssl refused). The caveat is in the README: the existing hand-built
assertions also caught all four, so none of them demonstrates reach the
suite did not already have. The OID gap and the sanitizer blindness are
what the fuzzer found that nothing else did.

216 -> 218 assertions.
